00:10:20.708 --> 00:10:24.232
- I definitely haven't already.

00:10:24.232 --> 00:10:26.948
Beme which is my technology startup.

00:10:26.948 --> 00:10:29.665
Here's some context for--

00:10:29.665 --> 00:10:31.104
- Link that up DRock,

00:10:31.104 --> 00:10:33.032
in the Facebook post,

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:34.216
in the YouTube post.

00:10:34.216 --> 00:10:36.120
Let's make sure everyone who watches--

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.627
- In fact, we'll put a link
below that if you click on it

00:10:38.627 --> 00:10:40.415
we'll automatically unlock Beme for you

00:10:40.415 --> 00:10:42.644
and you will automatically
be following Gary.

00:10:42.644 --> 00:10:44.130
- Oh.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:44.920
- You like that?

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:46.682
We're working, it's a new product
we got a new feature--

00:10:46.682 --> 00:10:48.478
- Take that (beep) (beep).
(laughs)

00:10:48.478 --> 00:10:50.901
- In the history of social networks,

00:10:50.901 --> 00:10:52.282
there's maybe been, what,

00:10:52.282 --> 00:10:53.370
what would you say?

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:54.305
How many have succeeded, eight?

00:10:54.305 --> 00:10:55.740
- Seven.
- Seven.

00:10:56.761 --> 00:10:57.830
That is the swimming pool

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.082
that we are currently wading around in.

00:11:00.082 --> 00:11:02.171
So to call yourself a success

00:11:02.171 --> 00:11:05.051
- And I think that we
define that as a success

00:11:05.051 --> 00:11:06.792
of like such meaningful scale,

00:11:06.792 --> 00:11:08.162
financial stability,

00:11:08.162 --> 00:11:10.136
looking like it's gonna
go in the right direction.

00:11:10.136 --> 00:11:11.413
- Social impact.

00:11:11.413 --> 00:11:12.666
- Impact, for sure.

00:11:12.666 --> 00:11:13.990
- To me, that is a success.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:16.567
And I mean it, when you can
count it on less than two hands

00:11:16.567 --> 00:11:18.518
how many companies have succeeded.

00:11:18.518 --> 00:11:21.350
It's not just catching a unicorn.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.787
It is the most, the rarest, hardest thing

00:11:24.787 --> 00:11:26.296
you could ever hope to accomplish

00:11:26.296 --> 00:11:27.712
in the space of technology.

00:11:27.712 --> 00:11:30.545
That's what we're trying to do,

00:11:30.545 --> 00:11:32.588
so have I considered it a success?

00:11:32.588 --> 00:11:34.005
Not even close.

00:11:34.005 --> 00:11:36.211
Ask me in four or five years.

00:11:36.211 --> 00:11:37.070
- Click the app.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:39.833
Yeah, I mean look,

00:11:39.833 --> 00:11:43.362
to me one can argue that
it's a success right now.

00:11:43.362 --> 00:11:45.057
The amount of people that I come across

00:11:45.057 --> 00:11:47.263
who are doing other things in their career

00:11:47.263 --> 00:11:50.026
that wanna go then make a app

00:11:50.026 --> 00:11:52.835
that has the ambition to
win the consumer web game

00:11:52.835 --> 00:11:54.902
is extraordinarily high.

00:11:54.902 --> 00:11:57.340
The amount that even saw the day of light,

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:00.869
even saw the day of light
with well financed funding.

00:12:00.869 --> 00:12:02.378
That's repetitive.

00:12:02.378 --> 00:12:03.446
With money.

00:12:03.446 --> 00:12:05.466
With all those things going in their way,

00:12:05.466 --> 00:12:06.627
is very small.

00:12:06.627 --> 00:12:08.671
Then to have that happen on top of which

00:12:08.671 --> 00:12:11.620
to have a very smart, you're
a tremendous marketer.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.523
You know, that means a lot to me.

00:12:13.523 --> 00:12:15.776
I'm sure you define yourself
in a lot of different ways

00:12:15.776 --> 00:12:18.376
but your marketing skills
are very high, I admire them.

00:12:18.376 --> 00:12:21.488
The amount of noise and
excitement that was generated

00:12:21.488 --> 00:12:24.135
felt amazing to me, then
you gotta back it up.

00:12:24.135 --> 00:12:25.551
So, now there's the next challenge.

00:12:25.551 --> 00:12:27.014
Now's the tough part.

00:12:27.014 --> 00:12:29.173
Is actually making the
product at that level.

00:12:29.173 --> 00:12:31.007
- Yeah, I mean some of the--

00:12:31.007 --> 00:12:31.820
- Tim?

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:32.795
Are you typing a new, I'm sorry.

00:12:32.795 --> 00:12:33.717
Don't do a new one.

00:12:33.717 --> 00:12:34.948
If it's done, it's done.

00:12:34.948 --> 00:12:35.807
- [Voiceover] Okay.

00:12:35.807 --> 00:12:36.838
- Cool, all right.

00:12:36.838 --> 00:12:38.161
- We just lost our Facebook feed.

00:12:38.161 --> 00:12:39.145
- No, no, okay.

00:12:39.145 --> 00:12:39.958
- But some,

00:12:39.958 --> 00:12:41.955
some of the greatest failures ever were

00:12:41.955 --> 00:12:45.089
a gigantic pile, an
aggregate of tiny successes.

00:12:45.089 --> 00:12:47.295
So I appreciate everything you just said

00:12:47.295 --> 00:12:51.103
and I really hold dear the tiny
successes we've had thus far

00:12:51.103 --> 00:12:53.077
- 60 seconds for everybody who's watching

00:12:53.077 --> 00:12:54.563
what it is, how you describe in 60.

00:12:54.563 --> 00:12:55.456
I know that's tough but--

00:12:55.456 --> 00:12:56.445
- What Beme is,

00:12:56.445 --> 00:12:58.788
is Beme is a way of sharing via video

00:12:58.788 --> 00:13:00.878
the tiny moments you experience in life

00:13:00.878 --> 00:13:03.200
and doing it in a way
that's absolutely dynamic

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:04.918
but doesn't interrupt the moment.

00:13:04.918 --> 00:13:06.311
And within this 60 second window

00:13:06.311 --> 00:13:08.285
I demonstrate to you
exactly how that happens

00:13:08.285 --> 00:13:09.771
and it looks like this.

00:13:09.771 --> 00:13:13.022
Like right now, I'm capturing
video of this entire set.

00:13:13.022 --> 00:13:15.135
Of Gary's beautiful face just like that

00:13:15.135 --> 00:13:16.435
and when you hear the noise

00:13:16.435 --> 00:13:17.387
(phone beeps)

00:13:17.387 --> 00:13:18.850
that means it's been shared
to all of my followers.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:19.964
That's what Beme is.

00:13:19.964 --> 00:13:21.055
- You know what I love?

00:13:21.055 --> 00:13:22.819
But you'll never be able to
see what you just shared.

00:13:22.819 --> 00:13:24.302
- Well, not until it's live in the network

00:13:24.302 --> 00:13:25.102
and everybody else can.

00:13:25.102 --> 00:13:26.277
And that's the whole idea.

00:13:26.277 --> 00:13:27.415
Is to remove the scrutiny.

00:13:27.415 --> 00:13:28.854
It's to remove sort of,

00:13:28.854 --> 00:13:30.666
controlling the image of yourself

00:13:30.666 --> 00:13:32.569
in life that you put out there.

00:13:32.569 --> 00:13:34.659
I'd like to say, Beme is not about sharing

00:13:34.659 --> 00:13:35.936
how the world sees you.

00:13:35.936 --> 00:13:37.933
It's sharing how you see the world.

00:13:37.933 --> 00:13:39.303
- And what's interesting about that is,

00:13:39.303 --> 00:13:40.812
what's really happening in social is

00:13:40.812 --> 00:13:46.252
platforms are showing who
you want to be to the world.

00:13:46.252 --> 00:13:49.032
We are all living in the most
PR'd version of ourselves.

00:13:49.032 --> 00:13:50.611
We have 15 year old girls

00:13:50.611 --> 00:13:52.213
running around America right now

00:13:52.213 --> 00:13:53.676
who are massive growth hackers

00:13:53.676 --> 00:13:56.044
who understand the speed
in which likes come in

00:13:56.044 --> 00:13:58.900
on an Instagram photo that
took them 17 minutes to take

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:01.547
and then they take it down
within the first 60 seconds

00:14:01.547 --> 00:14:04.844
because they don't like the
data that's coming back as fast.

00:14:04.844 --> 00:14:05.749
- Right.

00:14:05.749 --> 00:14:06.864
- And they reset.

00:14:06.864 --> 00:14:09.023
Literally, three hour dynamics
to pull off the one picture

00:14:09.023 --> 00:14:12.622
that's gonna capture the moment
of the concert you went to

00:14:12.622 --> 00:14:14.805
which is PR at its finest.

00:14:14.805 --> 00:14:16.987
It's an interesting dynamic.

00:14:16.987 --> 00:14:18.659
- Yeah, we're trying
to get away from that.

00:14:18.659 --> 00:14:20.401
Because I think our,

00:14:20.401 --> 00:14:22.908
the ethos, the principle behind
this, our mission statement

00:14:22.908 --> 00:14:27.064
is to promote understanding
by sharing perspective.

00:14:27.064 --> 00:14:29.131
And I think if you can tap into
other people's perspectives,

00:14:29.131 --> 00:14:31.546
you get a better idea as to
what the world is around you.

00:14:31.546 --> 00:14:32.498
- When I--

00:14:32.498 --> 00:14:33.496
I'm an investor.

00:14:33.496 --> 00:14:34.750
When I really got excited,

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.190
I'm still excited for this moment, is

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.511
the thought of like the
first time I go on stage

00:14:38.511 --> 00:14:40.996
and Beme to me is so exciting.

00:14:40.996 --> 00:14:43.782
Like literally, people
seeing what I get to see

00:14:43.782 --> 00:14:46.011
when I give these keynotes
versus watching me

00:14:46.011 --> 00:14:47.520
is an exciting moment for me.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.657
- Do you know, and we can
stop talking about Beme

00:14:49.657 --> 00:14:51.259
in a second, but last night

00:14:51.259 --> 00:14:53.163
a friend of my named Shon,

00:14:53.163 --> 00:14:54.904
Shonduras you know him from Snapchat.

00:14:54.904 --> 00:14:55.926
- Yeah, yeah.

00:14:55.926 --> 00:14:58.689
- Shon Bemed his daughter being born.

00:14:58.689 --> 00:14:59.780
- What?

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:00.686
(whistles)

00:15:00.686 --> 00:15:02.427
- I mean, my wife and I
watched it this morning in bed

00:15:02.427 --> 00:15:03.936
and we were both crying.

00:15:03.936 --> 00:15:05.817
I mean it was unbelievable.

00:15:05.817 --> 00:15:06.931
It wasn't the yucky stuff

00:15:06.931 --> 00:15:09.277
and it wasn't the stuff that
you would deem inappropriate.

00:15:09.277 --> 00:15:12.945
But it was his wife in the
chair, in the bed being anxious

00:15:12.945 --> 00:15:14.780
and in the next shot he's holding

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:16.266
his beautiful newborn baby girl.

00:15:16.266 --> 00:15:17.241
- That's cool.

00:15:17.241 --> 00:15:19.470
- And it was such an emotional,
such a real, raw thing.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.211
And that's we're hoping to accomplish.

00:15:21.211 --> 00:15:22.767
He didn't think, he just shared.

00:15:22.767 --> 00:15:23.465
- Love it.

00:15:23.465 --> 00:15:24.351
- Next question.

00:15:24.351 --> 00:15:26.423
- [Voiceover] Alex asks,

00:15:26.423 --> 00:15:28.884
"What advice would you give
to a high school student

00:15:28.884 --> 00:15:31.972
"struggling to decide what to
pursue as a future career?"

00:15:31.972 --> 00:15:33.830
- I've got an answer.

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:35.153
Get busy.

00:15:35.153 --> 00:15:37.057
Do something, do anything.

00:15:37.057 --> 00:15:38.497
And one of my favorite pieces of advice

00:15:38.497 --> 00:15:40.099
as to exactly what you should do,

00:15:40.099 --> 00:15:41.980
is do something you hate.

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:45.044
Like, I didn't find my
passion for entrepreneuralship

00:15:45.044 --> 00:15:47.436
and filmmaking and everything
that I've done in my career.

00:15:47.436 --> 00:15:49.712
I didn't find that by doing it.

00:15:49.712 --> 00:15:51.244
I found that by scrubbing pots

00:15:51.244 --> 00:15:53.589
in a really terrible seafood restaurant.

00:15:53.589 --> 00:15:56.190
Because when you spend 50
hours a week scrubbing pots,

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.838
it's 50 hours a week you're obsessing

00:15:57.838 --> 00:16:00.415
about what you wish you were doing.

00:16:00.415 --> 00:16:01.809
Sitting around playing video games

00:16:01.809 --> 00:16:03.271
in your parents' sweet house

00:16:03.271 --> 00:16:05.640
is the worst way to find
your mission in life.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.169
Do something, staying busy
is a really, really easy path

00:16:09.169 --> 00:16:11.909
to find something you truly
wanna invest yourself in.

00:16:11.909 --> 00:16:15.299
- I would also then say to recognize

00:16:15.299 --> 00:16:18.015
that playing video games in
your parents' sweet house

00:16:18.015 --> 00:16:21.800
is probably your outlet
to success in your career.

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.912
I would say that your
ultimate strengths and wants

00:16:24.912 --> 00:16:27.419
are the quickest gateway drug to upside

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:30.531
once you recognize that there's
something to be done there.

00:16:30.531 --> 00:16:33.154
Too many of you, in the world

00:16:33.154 --> 00:16:35.871
don't believe that the thing
they like doing the most

00:16:35.871 --> 00:16:37.473
has financial upside.

00:16:37.473 --> 00:16:39.145
We've drawn a line in the sand,

00:16:39.145 --> 00:16:41.142
that the thing we like is what we do when

00:16:41.142 --> 00:16:42.442
we have time to do it like.

00:16:42.442 --> 00:16:45.135
And the other places to
either make money or get by.

00:16:45.135 --> 00:16:46.761
So many people are in the other bucket

00:16:46.761 --> 00:16:48.548
are driven by, I'm gonna go into finance

00:16:48.548 --> 00:16:50.151
'cause that's where the money is.

00:16:50.151 --> 00:16:52.194
Or I just need enough money

00:16:52.194 --> 00:16:54.260
and then I'm gonna have
good work-life balance,

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:55.862
I'll be on the softball
team, I'll play video games.

00:16:55.862 --> 00:16:57.465
I really believe--

00:16:57.465 --> 00:16:59.624
I really believe that somebody
who's watching this right now

00:16:59.624 --> 00:17:02.944
who obsesses on being on six
soft ball teams right now,

00:17:02.944 --> 00:17:06.636
literally can make 127,000
dollars a year in ad revenue

00:17:06.636 --> 00:17:08.981
and live events and a
couple other sponsorships

00:17:08.981 --> 00:17:10.630
and selling a couple t-shirts

00:17:10.630 --> 00:17:13.810
by literally becoming
the authority on softball

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:15.156
in America.

00:17:15.156 --> 00:17:17.851
- My son who is a junior in high school

00:17:17.851 --> 00:17:19.337
is spending a good piece of his summer,

00:17:19.337 --> 00:17:21.147
including right now, today

00:17:21.147 --> 00:17:23.957
at a university in
Connecticut studying computer,

00:17:23.957 --> 00:17:27.881
computer science, specifically
focused on video game design.

00:17:27.881 --> 00:17:28.810
- Yeah.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:29.925
- Just to back up video playing.

00:17:29.925 --> 00:17:31.805
- Video game culture in 19--

00:17:31.805 --> 00:17:32.943
When we were kids.

00:17:32.943 --> 00:17:35.985
To think it was a mass industry.

00:17:35.985 --> 00:17:38.701
I mean E-Sport's gonna
be bigger than baseball

00:17:38.701 --> 00:17:40.513
in this country in 20 years.

00:17:40.513 --> 00:17:42.927
E-Sport's is gonna be a bigger business

00:17:42.927 --> 00:17:46.387
than major league baseball
in 20, 30 years from now.

00:17:46.387 --> 00:17:47.896
I'll go 30, I'm gonna hedge a little bit.

00:17:47.896 --> 00:17:49.011
In 30 years from now.

00:17:49.011 --> 00:17:50.891
I mean, that is incredible to me.

00:17:50.891 --> 00:17:52.052
India.

00:17:52.052 --> 00:17:53.887
- [Voiceover] Atiyya asks,

00:17:53.887 --> 00:17:56.307
"If you could swap out
one quality of your won

00:17:56.307 --> 00:18:00.224
"for one of Casey's, and vice
versa, what would it be?"

00:18:00.224 --> 00:18:01.339
- Mine's very easy.

00:18:01.339 --> 00:18:02.685
I don't know if you know me well enough

00:18:02.685 --> 00:18:04.612
to answer this question
but mine's very easy.

00:18:04.612 --> 00:18:08.026
I'm a wimp when it
comes to physical stuff.

00:18:08.026 --> 00:18:09.187
I don't ride skateboards.

00:18:09.187 --> 00:18:11.044
I don't surf or ski.

00:18:11.044 --> 00:18:13.923
I do very little risk

00:18:13.923 --> 00:18:15.015
adventure.

00:18:15.015 --> 00:18:15.990
I look at GoPro's, I'm like,

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.011
who gives a rat's ass?

00:18:17.011 --> 00:18:19.380
Like that's not even in
my consideration set.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:22.352
You know, one time jumped
off a 50 foot cliff

00:18:22.352 --> 00:18:23.977
in Jamaica, I still
can't believe I did that.

00:18:23.977 --> 00:18:24.906
(laughs)

00:18:24.906 --> 00:18:27.042
Because there was a lot of chicks there.

00:18:27.042 --> 00:18:29.434
Stuff was like peer
pressure, college stuff.

00:18:29.434 --> 00:18:31.547
I mean, like fuck it,
